      title: "Сатира, если никто не смеётся",
      text: "Every satirist has a nightmare, and it is not being sued. It is being believed.\n\nLast month a small satirical website published a story under the headline 'Government to tax daylight to fund lighting crisis'. The piece was a deadpan send-up of the way ministers announce unpopular policies: it quoted an imaginary spokesman insisting the measure was 'not without its challenges' and promising that 'the vast majority of hard-working families will barely notice the darkness'. Within hours it had been shared tens of thousands of times, largely by people who were furious. Several took it at face value. One regional politician issued a scathing statement condemning the policy. The website's editor, asked for comment, said only that the response had been 'slightly more than we expected'.\n\nEpisodes like this have prompted a minor panic about whether satire can survive the internet. The argument goes like this: satire depends on a shared sense of what is normal. When reality itself becomes absurd, the satirist loses the ground on which to stand. If real politicians say things that once would have been jokes, how is the reader supposed to tell the difference?\n\nIt is a seductive theory, but it is not entirely new. When Jonathan Swift suggested in 1729 that the Irish poor might ease their poverty by selling their children as food, some readers were genuinely appalled, apparently missing the point that the target was not the poor but the indifference of the wealthy. Satire has always relied on a degree of risk. The mock-serious tone that makes it funny is precisely what makes it possible to misread.\n\nWhat has changed is the speed and scale of the misreading. A pamphlet passed from hand to hand arrived with context: who printed it, where it was sold, who was laughing. A screenshot arrives with none. Stripped of its source, a tongue-in-cheek article looks exactly like a real one, which is, after all, the whole point of the joke.\n\nThere is a subtler problem too. Good satire punctures pomposity; it takes the self-important and cuts them down to size. But satire that is too gentle risks becoming mere entertainment, the kind of affectionate ribbing that powerful people are happy to share because it makes them look good-humoured. The most trenchant satirists, from Swift to the writers of today's late-night monologues, have understood that the joke should cost someone something. When a politician cheerfully reposts a sketch that lampoons him, it is worth asking whether the sketch has done its job.\n\nNone of this means satirists should start labelling their work. A joke that comes with instructions is no longer a joke. But it may mean that readers have to rediscover an old and slightly unfashionable skill: pausing for three seconds before reacting, and asking who is really the butt of the joke.",
