      title: "Пять слов, чтобы продать историю",
      text: "Ask any newspaper sub-editor about their job and you will probably hear the same complaint: readers think reporters write the headlines. They do not. In most newsrooms, the headline is the work of someone who has never met the source, never seen the documents and has roughly ninety seconds to sum up a two-thousand-word investigation in five words that will fit across three columns.\n\nIt is a peculiar craft, with its own grammar and its own vocabulary. Headlines live in a permanent present tense, so a resignation that happened yesterday becomes 'Minister quits'. The future is signalled by an infinitive, as in 'Council to axe library'. Articles vanish, auxiliaries disappear, and long words are replaced by short, punchy ones that ordinary people never use in conversation. Nobody in real life says that the government has 'mulled a curb' on anything, yet a headline that reads 'Ministers mull curb on energy prices' is instantly understood.\n\nThe vocabulary matters because it carries judgement. Consider the difference between 'Prime Minister responds to critics', 'PM hits back at critics' and 'Embattled PM lashes out'. All three could describe the same speech. The first is neutral; the second suggests energy and confidence; the third paints a picture of a leader under siege, losing control. A sub-editor choosing between them is not just summarising. She is deciding how the reader will feel before they have read a single sentence of the story.\n\nThe same is true of reporting verbs. 'Firm denies pollution claims' and 'Firm dismisses pollution claims' sound similar, but the second implies arrogance. 'Experts warn' implies that the experts are right; 'Experts claim' quietly invites the reader to doubt them. Lawyers pay close attention to words like 'allegedly' and 'purported', because a headline that states an accusation as fact can expose the paper to a libel action even if the article underneath is carefully balanced.\n\nAll of this has become more complicated online. A print headline only has to persuade someone who has already bought the paper to keep reading. A web headline has to compete with millions of others for a single click, and the temptation to sensationalise is constant. The result is the familiar genre of clickbait: 'You won't believe what this mayor did next' or 'Scientists are terrified of this one discovery'. Such headlines promise far more than the story can deliver, and readers, who are not stupid, have learnt to distrust them.\n\nSome newspapers have responded by writing two headlines for every story: a sharp, compact one for the front page and a longer, more descriptive one for search engines. The front-page splash still needs to grab attention above the fold. But the web version increasingly reads like a sentence a human being might actually say.\n\nThe best sub-editors argue that the principles have not changed. A good headline is accurate, it is fair to the people it names, and it makes you want to read on. A bad one does only the last of those, and that, they insist, is not a headline at all. It is an advertisement.",
