      model: "Report: Coverage of the Riverside Factory Closure\n\nIntroduction\nThis report compares how three outlets covered last week's announcement that the Riverside packaging factory will close, with the loss of 400 jobs. It assesses the objectivity of each and makes recommendations for our own coverage.\n\nThe Daily Herald\nThe Herald's headline, 'Bosses axe 400 jobs in greed scandal', was the most sensational of the three. The word 'greed' is not supported by anything in the article, which merely notes that the owners allegedly paid themselves large bonuses last year. The reporting verbs follow the same pattern: union leaders 'reveal' and 'warn', while the company only 'claims'. Readers are told what to think before they are given the facts.\n\nThe Northern Times\nThe Times took a far more cautious line: 'Riverside factory to close, 400 jobs lost'. The article quoted both sides at length. It reported that the managing director conceded the decision had been 'handled badly' and that he dismissed suggestions that the site had been run down deliberately. The tone was balanced, if somewhat dry.\n\nNewsNow website\nThe online outlet chose 'You won't believe why this factory is shutting'. This is clickbait in its purest form: the story itself contains no surprising revelation, and the headline gives no useful information at all. It may attract clicks, but it undermines trust.\n\nRecommendations\nFirst, our headlines should state the key facts plainly, as the Times did. Second, sub-editors should check that reporting verbs reflect what sources actually said rather than our opinion of them. Finally, the web team should write descriptive headlines rather than mysterious ones; readers who feel tricked rarely return.\n\nConclusion\nAccuracy and fairness need not make a headline dull. They simply make it trustworthy."
