    title: "«Правда обычно скучна»: интервью",
    text: "Margaret Doyle spent thirty-five years as an investigative reporter, exposing everything from rigged public contracts to a pharmaceutical company that concealed the side effects of a popular drug. Now retired, she teaches journalism students and writes, occasionally, for a satirical magazine. We asked her about the state of the profession.\n\nYou've said that most investigations fail. That seems a strange thing for a famous investigator to admit.\n\nIt's simply true. For every story that ran, there were four or five that died quietly in a drawer. Sometimes the source got cold feet. Sometimes the documents turned out to be less damning than we'd hoped. And sometimes we were just wrong. Young reporters find that hard to accept. They imagine that if they dig long enough they'll always find something. Occasionally the reason you can't find any wrongdoing is that there isn't any.\n\nWhat separates a good investigative reporter from a merely persistent one?\n\nScepticism, mostly about yourself. Anyone can be suspicious of a politician. The difficult thing is being suspicious of your own theory, especially when you've spent six months on it and your editor is asking where the story is. I used to keep a list headed 'Reasons I might be wrong'. If the list got longer than the evidence, I stopped.\n\nYou now write satire. Isn't that a strange career move?\n\nLess strange than it looks. Satire and investigation are cousins. Both start from the suspicion that what powerful people say is not quite what they mean. The investigator proves it with documents; the satirist proves it with a joke. Frankly, the satirist often reaches more people. I once spent a year on an exposé about a charity's accounts, and it was read by perhaps forty thousand people. A two-minute sketch based on the same story was watched by three million. I'd be lying if I said that didn't sting a little.\n\nHow do you feel about the way news is presented now? Headlines in particular.\n\nI'm not one of those people who think everything was wonderful in the past. Newspapers have always oversold stories. But I do think the incentives have got worse. When a headline is judged purely by clicks, the pressure to exaggerate is enormous. I've seen careful pieces of mine appear online under headlines I'd never have approved, with a reporting verb changed from 'said' to 'admitted', which turns a statement into a confession. That's not a small thing. It's the difference between informing someone and accusing them.\n\nWhat would you say to a student who wants to do the kind of work you did?\n\nThat the truth is usually boring. It lives in spreadsheets, company registers and planning applications, not in car parks at midnight. If you can find a spreadsheet exciting, you might be good at this. And learn some law. Not because you should be frightened of lawyers, but because knowing exactly what you can prove is what lets you be brave about everything else.",
