    minWords: 300, use: ["in the public interest", "satire", "allegedly", "not without"],
    model: "Few freedoms are defended as loudly, or abused as often, as the freedom of the press. The question of whether journalists and satirists should be allowed to expose and mock anyone they choose is therefore not without its complications. In my view, the answer depends less on who the target is than on why the target has been chosen.\n\nThe case for broad freedom is strong. Investigative journalism exists precisely to reveal what powerful people would prefer to hide. Without reporters willing to follow a paper trail through company records and council minutes, corruption would flourish unseen. The same is true of opinion writing: a columnist who cannot criticise a government without fear of punishment is not a columnist but a press officer. Satire, too, has a long and honourable history of puncturing the pomposity of rulers. A society in which nobody may laugh at the prime minister is rarely a free one.\n\nHowever, freedom is not the same as licence. Not every story that interests the public is in the public interest. A minister who allegedly accepts bribes is a legitimate target; the minister's teenage daughter is not. Private individuals who suddenly find themselves in the news, perhaps because of a single unfortunate video, have none of the resources that politicians use to defend themselves. Mocking them is not brave. It is closer to bullying.\n\nThere is also the problem of accuracy. Satire depends on the audience recognising exaggeration, and in an age when stories are shared without context, a joke can easily be taken at face value. Investigations, meanwhile, can destroy reputations if they are rushed or poorly sourced.\n\nOn balance, I believe journalists and satirists should be free to target anyone who holds power, provided they are accurate and fair. Their duty is to aim upwards. When they turn their attention to people with no power at all, the defence of free expression becomes much harder to sustain."
